topic|Bitcoin|https://www.coindesk.com/m.text:
{
  "stance": "bearish",
  "view": "Bitcoin is under pressure. It fell below $81,000 and nearly $1 billion in leveraged longs were liquidated. Spot ETFs saw $487.1 million in net outflows on Wednesday, and rising long-term Treasury yields are pulling capital away from risk assets. The AI security warning and the US government's $770 million move to Coinbase Prime add noise, but the outflow and yield data are what matter.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Bitcoin falls below $81K, triggering nearly $1B in liquidations",
      "essay": "Bitcoin dropped below $81,000 on Thursday, touching an intraday low of $80,900 before recovering to around $81,100, down 2.8% over 24 hours. The move triggered $974 million in crypto liquidations, with $896 million from long positions, according to CoinGlass. Ethereum led with $311 million in liquidations, followed by Bitcoin at $238 million.\n\nThe selloff spilled into crypto-linked equities. Strategy, Coinbase and Block each fell over 2%. Bitcoin miners expanding into AI data center infrastructure took a harder hit: Riot Platforms dropped more than 9%, Cipher Mining fell 7.9%, CleanSpark slid 7.1%, and Core Scientific lost about 2%. All four touched their lowest levels since April. The pullback reflects investor reassessment of capital-intensive AI infrastructure businesses as rising yields make external financing more expensive.\n\nRetail sentiment on Stocktwits was bearish, with BTC the top-trending ticker. The liquidation wave shows how much leveraged long positioning had built up, and the forced selling is now clearing it out.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spot bitcoin ETFs see $487M net outflows as Q4 range forecast",
      "essay": "US spot bitcoin ETFs recorded $487.1 million in net outflows on Wednesday, reversing $118.8 million in inflows the previous day. BlackRock's IBIT led withdrawals at $207.7 million, followed by Fidelity's FBTC at $105.2 million and Ark Invest/21Shares' ARKB at $101.7 million. Over the past five trading sessions, the funds have seen $165.6 million in net outflows, following a prior nine-day inflow streak of $3.1 billion.\n\nQCP Capital's base case for Q4 puts bitcoin in an $80,000 to $90,000 range. The firm sees $80,000 to $82,000 as a buy area and would reduce exposure at $88,000 to $90,000 if ETF inflows have not picked up. QCP expects ETF inflows to remain positive but inconsistent, with uncertainty around the Clarity Act weighing on the market. Its bull case puts bitcoin above $100,000 on sustained ETF inflows and a Fed pause; its bear case sees a drop below $68,000 to $70,000 if Middle East fighting intensifies and oil prices push the Fed to hike further.\n\nGlassnode noted bitcoin fell from a sell wall at $86,500 toward large buy orders, the biggest around $81,000, which may cushion further declines. The 30-year Treasury yield climbed 4 basis points to 5.71% and the 10-year to 5.32%, ahead of a $22 billion 30-year bond auction.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "headline": "US government moves $770M bitcoin to Coinbase Prime custody",
      "essay": "US government-linked wallets transferred 9,261 bitcoin worth approximately $770 million to Coinbase Prime on Tuesday and Wednesday, according to Galaxy Research. Roughly half came from Bitfinex hacker seizures and another portion from known Binance seizures. An additional 2,456 bitcoin came from previously unidentified holdings and may represent new law enforcement seizures.\n\nCoinbase Prime has served as a custody provider for the US Marshals since 2024, so the transfer does not mean the government intends to sell. An executive order from President Trump said the US would establish a strategic Bitcoin reserve and commit to never selling bitcoin it holds. The government has not provided the direct accounting of its crypto holdings that the order called for.\n\nThe more interesting detail is the 2,456 bitcoin from unidentified holdings, which may signal a seizure not yet announced. For holders, the transfer itself is custody, not a sale.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Gold|https://www.mitrade.com/au/in.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Gold is caught between a hawkish Fed and safe-haven demand, so the flow is mixed. The Fed minutes showed most officials backing another hike by year-end, and Waller said more hikes are likely, which pressures gold. But Trump ruled out an attack on Iran before the midterms, and central banks keep buying. Price sits near $4,120, below the 100-day average, with no clear direction.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Fed signals more hikes, keeping gold under pressure",
      "essay": "Gold edged up to around $4,140 in early Asian trading, rebounding from a two-month low, but the Fed's hawkish tone caps gains. Minutes from the last meeting showed policymakers united behind September's hike, with most judging another increase appropriate by year-end. Governor Waller said more hikes are likely needed, though he left room for a pause in October. Traders price a 17.7% chance of an October hike and an 81.3% chance of a December move, per CME FedWatch. Higher rates raise the opportunity cost of holding gold, which pays no yield.\n\nTechnically, gold trades below its 100-day simple moving average near $4,265 and the Bollinger middle band around $4,240, with the RSI at 39.99, below neutral. Support sits near $4,060. Analysts at Scotiabank said gold looks vulnerable after breaking under $4,100, while copper holds near record highs. For gold holders, the Fed path remains the main headwind.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trump rules out Iran strike before midterms, easing haven demand",
      "essay": "President Trump said the US won't attack Iran before the November midterm elections, adding that Washington is having productive discussions with Iran. The remarks followed a report that the White House was considering striking Iran ahead of the midterms. The shift reduces immediate safe-haven demand for gold, which often rises on geopolitical tension. Gold was trading near $4,117 an ounce as of 9 a.m. Eastern, up $99 from yesterday but only $82 above a year ago.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Central banks keep buying gold, led by China",
      "essay": "China's central bank added about 23 tonnes of gold in September, its 23rd straight month of buying. Central banks worldwide bought a net 39 tonnes in August, according to the World Gold Council. Russia's central bank has sold 56 tonnes this year, which Peter Schiff said is what reserves are for when a country at war needs cash. Schiff also said rising bond yields are bullish for gold because bond investors losing money will move into gold. The 10-year Treasury yield touched 5.35%, its highest since 2002. For gold holders, steady official-sector demand offers a floor under prices even as the Fed tightens.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

topic|HAL|https://www.outlookbusiness.co.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed flow. GE shipped four F404-IN20 engines in September, taking HAL's total to 14 and easing the Tejas Mk1A bottleneck. Honeywell expects to deliver 16 TPE331-12B engines for the HTT-40 ahead of the mid-2029 schedule. Against that, a retired Air Marshal says HAL has lost the IAF's faith, and the IAF still flies about 29 fighter squadrons against a sanctioned 42.5.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "GE ships four more F404 engines to HAL for Tejas Mk1A",
      "essay": "GE Aerospace shipped four F404-IN20 engines to HAL in September 2026, taking total deliveries to 14. GE sent three in August after handing over six by the end of the 2025-26 financial year, and expects to deliver eight more by the end of 2026.\n\nThe engines come from a $716 million contract signed in 2021 for 99 units, enough to power the first batch of 83 Tejas Mk1A jets ordered by the IAF. GE plans to raise its delivery rate to roughly 30 engines a year from next year and clear the backlog by 2028. HAL has built about 30 Mk1A airframes that are complete or nearly complete but waiting on engines.\n\nFor HAL, the schedule matters because the Tejas Mk1A is meant to replace retired aircraft. The IAF operates around 29 fighter squadrons against a sanctioned strength of 42.5. If GE holds the pace, HAL can fit its parked airframes, run flight tests and start handing over combat-ready jets in the coming months.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Honeywell expects HTT-40 engine deliveries ahead of schedule",
      "essay": "Honeywell Aerospace expects to deliver all 16 TPE331-12B engines for HAL's HTT-40 basic trainer ahead of the original mid-2029 deadline. Four engines are already delivered, with four more due by the end of 2026, taking the total to eight, and deliveries rising through 2027 and 2028.\n\nThe engines sit under a contract signed in July 2022 worth more than $100 million, covering 88 engines and kits plus maintenance and support. Honeywell supplies the first 16 directly, after which technology transfer lets HAL assemble the engines in India and expand its repair and overhaul work. Honeywell worked through supply-chain problems after a fire destroyed a supplier's facility, adding alternative sources and placing its own staff at supplier sites.\n\nHoneywell is also pitching the TPE331 family for India's unmanned aircraft and discussing navigation, electronic warfare and autonomy work with Indian firms, including startups. It has around 3,600 employees in India, nearly 3,000 of them engineers, and has invested more than $150 million over three decades in local infrastructure.",
      "sources": [1, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retired Air Marshal says HAL has lost the IAF's faith",
      "essay": "Air Marshal Choudhury (R) said HAL has lost the IAF's faith, pointing to the Tejas programme's delays. The criticism lands as the IAF works with around 29 fighter squadrons against a sanctioned 42.5, after the MiG-21 retirement.\n\nThe Tejas first flew on January 4, 2001. Twenty-five years on, the IAF has not re-equipped the squadrons losing MiG-21s at the pace needed. HAL has missed manufacturing timelines and blames the IAF for changing Air Staff Qualitative Requirements, the design benchmarks, while the IAF blames HAL. The article argues HAL's lines cannot build the 36-40 combat aircraft a year the Air Chief wants, and that new private production lines are the way out.\n\nFor HAL, the pressure is on execution, not orders. The IAF's own leadership pipeline, drawn largely from fighter ranks, also draws scrutiny in the piece.",
      "sources": [2, 4]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Infoedge/Naukri|https://www.saasri.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed Q2 update. Recruitment billings rose 12.6% to ₹613.8 crore, with underlying growth of 14-15% once renewal timing is stripped out. 99acres accelerated to 21.2% growth and matchmaking rose 23.1%. But Shiksha fell 27.2% as AI search cuts referral traffic, and the stock slipped 3%. Growth is steady, not accelerating.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Info Edge Q2 billings up 12.8%, recruitment grows 12.6%",
      "essay": "Info Edge reported standalone billings of ₹822.3 crore for Q2 FY27, up 12.8% from ₹729 crore a year earlier. For the first half, billings rose 13.5% to ₹1,559.3 crore.\n\nRecruitment, the largest segment and home to Naukri, grew 12.6% to ₹613.8 crore. Management said underlying growth was closer to 14-15% once it adjusted for the timing of customer renewals, which can shift reported figures between quarters. Adoption of its B2B AI products, AI-Rex and Talent Pulse, kept rising in both new sign-ups and revenue contribution.\n\nThe market still marked the stock down 3%. Reported growth sits below the 30%+ ARR expansion of high-growth pure-play SaaS firms, and the figures are unaudited, ahead of formal results. For a holder, the read is steady rather than accelerating: the core recruitment engine is fine, but it is not yet showing the AI-driven step-up in growth that would justify a higher multiple.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3, 4, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "99acres growth accelerates to 21.2%, matchmaking up 23.1%",
      "essay": "The real estate platform 99acres grew billings 21.2% year-on-year to ₹148.3 crore, up from 16.5% growth in Q1 FY27. For H1, 99acres billings rose 19.2% to ₹258.4 crore. The company said it held its share of category traffic, with web time-share at 49% and app time-share at 59% in July and August 2026, citing SimilarWeb.\n\nMatchmaking also grew. Jeevansathi billings rose 18.4% to ₹39.7 crore, and Aisle jumped 40%. Combined, the matchmaking vertical was up 23.1%.\n\nThese two segments are now the faster-growing parts of the portfolio, and they are pulling the blended growth rate up while recruitment plods along at low double digits. The traffic-share numbers matter because they suggest 99acres is not losing ground to rivals even as it raises prices. For a holder, this is the part of the update that supports the case for owning the stock.",
      "sources": [2, 3, 4, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Shiksha billings fall 27.2% as AI search cuts traffic",
      "essay": "Shiksha, the education platform, saw billings drop 27.2% year-on-year to ₹20.5 crore from ₹28.1 crore. For H1, billings fell 24.5% to ₹55.1 crore.\n\nInfo Edge blamed the decline on AI-powered search tools that answer user queries directly, cutting the referral traffic that used to reach Shiksha from search engines. The company said it is adapting the business model and adding new offerings to reduce dependence on search, but expects billings growth to stay under pressure in the interim, with the transition playing out over the medium term.\n\nShiksha is small, so the direct hit to group billings is limited. The signal is the bigger issue: a search-dependent vertical can lose a quarter of its billings in a year when AI search intercepts the traffic. That is a warning for any part of the portfolio that relies on organic search rather than a logged-in, habitual user base.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3, 4, 6]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Jio Platforms|https://www.tradingv.text:
{
  "stance": "bullish",
  "view": "Jio's IPO is on track with a possible ₹11-12 lakh crore valuation and a ₹37,700 crore issue size. Akash Ambani calls it a 'great responsibility' and hints at sharing value with retail investors. Jio will stay the most affordable operator even as Airtel raises postpaid tariffs by ₹50. The listing could also narrow Reliance's holding-company discount, which now sits at 36%.",
  "stories": [
    {
    "headline": "Jio IPO nears with ₹11-12 lakh crore valuation target",
    "essay": "Jio Platforms is moving toward what could be India's largest-ever IPO, with a proposed valuation of ₹11 lakh crore ($114 billion) and a possible issue size of ₹37,700 crore. The company filed its draft prospectus in June and received the markets regulator's final observations on August 28. Akash Ambani, Chairman of Reliance Jio and MD of Jio Platforms, told CNBC-TV18 and ET that the listing is a 'great responsibility' and the 'biggest moment' for the company, with the goal of sharing value with Indian citizens, including retail investors. He said the valuation and investor details would be known at the right time. The IPO could open as soon as October 21, according to reports. Motilal Oswal noted the offering could re-rate not just Reliance Industries but also Bharti Airtel. For Jio, the listing marks the start of its next growth phase, with a focus on an indigenous technology stack and affordable solutions for India's billion-plus users.",
    "sources": [1, 3, 5]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Jio stays affordable as Airtel raises postpaid tariffs by ₹50",
    "essay": "Bharti Airtel raised its postpaid tariffs across the board by ₹50, effective October 8, 2026, with existing subscribers moved to the nearest higher plan within a month. The hike translates to a 3-11% increase. Analysts expect telecom operators to raise tariffs by 12-15% over the next 4-5 months. Akash Ambani said Jio will always remain the most affordable player while delivering quality service. Jio's average revenue per user (ARPU) rose 3.3% year-on-year to ₹215.6 in the second quarter of the current financial year, while Airtel's ARPU increased 5.6% to ₹264. Ambani also said Jio is becoming a technology creator, not just a buyer, showcasing its own end-to-end satellite stack at India Mobile Congress. Jio is building a constellation of over 1,600 low-Earth orbit satellites with 23 ground stations to compete with Starlink, which has over 11,000 satellites. For Jio, the tariff gap with Airtel may pressure ARPU in the near term, but the affordability stance could help retain and grow its 50 crore subscriber base.",
    "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Reliance trades at 36% discount to Jio stake value",
    "essay": "Reliance Industries shares imply a roughly 36% discount to the value of its two-thirds stake in Jio Platforms, according to Nimish Maheshwari of Beat The Street. He sees about 25% as a sustainable gap after Jio lists, in line with how the market values Bharti Airtel's holdings in its listed subsidiaries. Jio may seek a valuation of about ₹11 trillion ($114 billion) in its IPO, which would value Reliance's stake at about ₹7.3 trillion, or 45% of the parent's current market value. Brokerages including Motilal Oswal, Yes Securities and Nuvama value the stake at ₹331 to 450 per Reliance share, implying it accounts for 27% to 37% of the parent's share price. Reliance shares have fallen 23% this year, compared with a 14% decline in the Nifty 50. Thea Jamison of Change Global Investment said how Reliance monetizes its Jio stake could be a significant source of value creation. For Jio, the listing will provide transparent price discovery, which could help narrow the holding-company discount and re-rate the parent.",
    "sources": [6]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Flipkart|https://www.mymobileindia.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Flipkart's Big Billion Days is live, with early access on October 8 and the main sale opening October 9. The iPhone 17 256GB is listed at Rs 84,999, or Rs 79,999 with an ICICI Bank card, while the LG 55UR7500 4K TV sits at Rs 35,899. Flipkart Minutes also hired Dibyendu Sen as associate director for brand partnerships and monetisation. The news is mostly promotional and personnel, not a shift in the business.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Flipkart Big Billion Days opens with iPhone and TV discounts",
      "essay": "Flipkart's Big Billion Days sale is live, with early access on October 8 for Flipkart Plus, VIP, BLACK and select credit card members, and the main sale opening to everyone on October 9. The iPhone 17 with 256GB is listed at Rs 84,999, down from Rs 99,900, and an extra Rs 5,000 ICICI Bank instant discount brings the effective price to Rs 79,999. Without any exchange, the phone costs Rs 82,999 after a Rs 2,000 card discount, which is Rs 99 above its 2025 launch price of Rs 82,900. Flipkart also shows the LG 55UR7500 55-inch 4K smart TV at Rs 35,899, down from a listed Rs 71,990. The TV runs LG's webOS platform with an Alpha5 AI Processor 4K Gen6 and 20W speakers. Bank and exchange offers can cut the checkout price further, but eligibility varies. For Flipkart, the sale is the main festive demand event, and the headline discounts are doing the work of pulling shoppers in.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Flipkart Minutes hires Dibyendu Sen for brand partnerships",
      "essay": "Dibyendu Sen has joined Flipkart Minutes as associate director for brand partnerships and monetisation. He announced the move on LinkedIn. Sen comes from Criteo, where he spent over two years and most recently was senior manager for enterprise sales, working on retail media monetisation and advertising across Blinkit, Swiggy Instamart and Zepto. He previously spent over two years at Flipkart in senior manager roles covering FMCG, home and grocery ads, and nearly two years at Paytm Ads handling FMCG, D2C and F&B. He started in sales at Zee Entertainment Enterprises and later spent nearly six years at Star TV Network. The hire points to Flipkart building out advertising revenue inside its quick-commerce arm, a business where rival platforms already sell retail media.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Safari suitcase deals compared across Flipkart and Amazon",
      "essay": "Safari suitcases are heavily discounted on both Flipkart and Amazon, and the seller matters more than the discount percentage. Flipkart lists the Magnum Zeno and Magnum Fury medium suitcases at Rs 1,449 against a displayed MRP of Rs 9,370, and the Astra Neo set at Rs 3,799, or Rs 3,609 with an additional offer. Several of those listings are sold by SafariIndustriesLtd itself. On Amazon, a Safari Pentagon two-piece set was selling for Rs 2,989 against a displayed MRP of Rs 19,998, roughly 85% off, but that listing was fulfilled by Amazon and sold by RetailEZ Pvt Ltd. Safari's warranty covers manufacturing defects and excludes wear and tear, scratches, dents, zip damage and transit mishandling, and buyers must keep the original invoice. For Flipkart, the takeaway is that its marketplace still wins price checks when the brand sells directly on it.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

et|https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/.text:
{
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Startups will crown new champions today at 12th ET Startup Awards in Bengaluru",
      "essay": "The twelfth edition of The Economic Times Startup Awards takes place in Bengaluru today, with Union commerce and industry minister Piyush Goyal as guest of honour. Goyal is expected to speak about the government's efforts to deepen and expand the country's emerging business ecosystem.\n\nGroww cofounder and CEO Lalit Keshre and Meesho cofounder and CEO Vidit Aatrey will reflect on their journeys from young upstarts to leaders of multi-billion-dollar enterprises, as once-fledgling startups increasingly make the transition to publicly listed companies. Accel founding partner Prashanth Prakash will discuss India's hard-tech ambitions with Kshitij Khandelwal, cofounder and CTO of spacetech startup Pixxel; Laina Emmanuel, cofounder and CEO of BrainSight AI; and Dheemanth Nagaraj, cofounder and CEO of AI semiconductor startup Agrani Labs.\n\nThe high-powered jury led by Wipro chairman Rishad Premji met in Bengaluru in August to pick winners across eight categories. Watch for the winner list and any policy signals from Goyal's address.",
      "word_count": 168
    },
    {
      "headline": "TCS profit up 15%, revenue by 11%",
      "essay": "Tata Consultancy Services reported a 15% on-year growth in consolidated net profit for the second quarter of FY27, at Rs 13,884 crore, versus Rs 12,075 crore a year ago. Revenue from operations rose 11% YoY to Rs 73,188 crore, compared with Rs 65,799 crore in the year-ago period. The board declared a dividend of Rs 12 per share for FY27.\n\nAnnualised AI revenue reached $3.1 billion in Q2 and crossed 10% of earnings, according to executive director, president and chief operating officer Aarthi Subramanian, who said AI momentum remained strong with demand for AI-native solutions, AI-led enterprise transformation, autonomous global business services, and cybersecurity. TCS recorded $9.6 billion in total contract value from deals during the quarter.\n\nIt signed a five-year partnership with Porsche AG and proposed to acquire Porsche's Germany-based management and IT consulting subsidiary, MHP. TCS will also take over Best Buy's India GCC (global capability centre) and transform it into an AI Capability Center. Watch whether AI revenue keeps pace as a share of earnings.",
      "word_count": 172
    },
    {
      "headline": "AI will fuel another wave of startup creation in two years: AWS ventures chief",
      "essay": "Artificial intelligence is lowering barriers to building companies and is likely to fuel another wave of startup creation over the next two years, as founders build products faster and operate with smaller teams, according to Jason Bennett, vice president and global head of startups and venture capital at Amazon Web Services.\n\n\"We have more startups being created today than there were two years ago. And I suspect if you look out two years from now, you'll see a further increase,\" Bennett said. AI-native startups are showing faster revenue growth and gaining market traction earlier than traditional startups. An AWS study with Strand Partners found AI-native startups growing revenues 167% year-on-year and are 5.5 times more likely to reach $1 million in earnings than non-AI-native peers. \"Some companies are getting to hundreds of millions in Annual Recurring Revenue (ARR) very quickly,\" Bennett said.\n\nAWS is increasingly supporting startups beyond cloud infrastructure, helping with go-to-market and enterprise customer acquisition through AWS Marketplace. \"It's not enough for us just to provide credits or to have a platform of services… We also have to help them drive engagement and win customers,\" Bennett said. Watch whether the predicted wave shows up in funding and formation data.",
      "word_count": 190
    }
  ],
  "other": [
    {
      "headline": "DailyObjects raises Rs 332 crore",
      "line": "Lifestyle accessories brand DailyObjects has raised Rs 332 crore led by Xponentia Capital Partners, Anicut Capital, and Axiom Asia Private Capital, valuing the company at Rs 1,050 crore post-money."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Groww adds 1,00,961 active clients",
      "line": "Online brokerage Groww added 1,00,961 active clients in September even as the industry's overall client base grew just 0.35% month-on-month, according to NSE data."
    }
  ]
}

topic|Bitcoin|https://www.coindesk.com/m.text:
{
  "stance": "bearish",
  "view": "Bitcoin is under pressure. It fell to about $82,300, roughly 4% below Tuesday's high near $86,600, and spot ETFs saw $487.1 million in net outflows on Wednesday. The 30-year Treasury yield rose to 5.71% and Fed minutes showed all 19 officials backed the last rate hike, with most seeing another by year-end. Derivatives show de-risking rather than fresh shorts, so the selling is less forced but sellers still hold the edge.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Bitcoin slides as ETF outflows and rising yields weigh",
      "essay": "Bitcoin dipped to about $82,300 during Asian hours before recovering to around $82,800, roughly 4% below Tuesday's high near $86,600. The CoinDesk 100 lost close to 2% over 24 hours.\n\nU.S. spot bitcoin ETFs recorded $487.1 million in net outflows on Wednesday, reversing $118.8 million in inflows the previous day. BlackRock's IBIT led with $207.7 million, followed by Fidelity's FBTC at $105.2 million and Ark Invest/21Shares' ARKB at $101.7 million. The funds have seen $165.6 million in net outflows over the past five trading sessions after a prior $3.1 billion nine-day inflow streak.\n\nBond markets added pressure. The 30-year Treasury yield climbed 4 basis points to 5.71% and the 10-year to 5.32%. Minutes of the Fed's September meeting showed all 19 officials backed last month's rate increase, with most seeing another as likely appropriate by year-end.\n\nDerivatives positioning shows sellers still in control but with less forced selling. The 24-hour taker long/short ratio is 48% to 52%. Crypto futures open interest is down 1% at $150 billion, and liquidations fell to $400 million from $548 million yesterday. Notional open interest has fallen in BTC, ETH, HYPE, XRP and DOGE by as much as or more than their spot prices, meaning traders are not adding new bearish bets. Bitcoin's one-week put-call skew rose to 10%, a bias for downside protection.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "headline": "US government moves $1.5 billion in seized bitcoin to Coinbase",
      "essay": "Wallets linked to the U.S. government moved 17,795 bitcoin worth $1.5 billion over the past three days, according to Arkham Intelligence and Galaxy Research. About 9,261 BTC, valued at around $770 million, went to Coinbase Prime on October 7-8. A separate transfer of 12,267 bitcoin, worth about $1.01 billion, left a wallet holding funds seized in the 2016 Bitfinex hack and went to a new, unlabeled address. No exchange deposit was recorded for that move, a pattern more consistent with wallet reshuffling than a sale.\n\nCoinbase Prime is the institutional arm of Coinbase and has served as a custody provider for the US Marshals Service since 2024. A March 2025 executive order directed that forfeited bitcoin be placed into a Strategic Bitcoin Reserve and said it should not be sold. The government still holds about $25.5 billion worth of crypto, Arkham shows, or an estimated 319,000 to 325,000 BTC.\n\nThe transfers sparked short-term speculation, but the stated policy points to custody management rather than liquidation. The victim compensation exception in the executive order is the detail to watch, since some seized coins have identifiable victims and returning funds could require moving or converting assets.",
      "sources": [3, 4, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "QCP sees $80,000-$90,000 Q4 range for bitcoin",
      "essay": "QCP Capital forecasts bitcoin will trade between $80,000 and $90,000 in its base case for the fourth quarter. The firm identified $80,000 to $82,000 as an area to buy and said it would reduce exposure around $88,000 to $90,000 if ETF inflows have not picked up. It expects ETF inflows to remain positive but inconsistent, with oil prices elevated but stable and uncertainty around the Clarity Act weighing on the market.\n\nQCP's bull case puts bitcoin above $100,000, supported by sustained ETF inflows, growth in stablecoin supply, a Fed pause on weaker employment data, a softer dollar, lower real yields, and progress on the Clarity Act. Its bear case sees bitcoin below $68,000 to $70,000 if Middle East fighting intensifies, oil prices rise, and the Fed hikes further, along with renewed ETF outflows or a corporate holder forced to sell.\n\nGlassnode said bitcoin fell back from a sell wall at $86,500 toward large buy orders, the biggest around $81,000, which it expects to cushion further declines. Kraken Chief Economist Thomas Perfumo said previous cycles took 12 to 13 months from high to bottom, but this cycle's 54% drawdown to its July low is smaller than the 70% to 85% typical declines. He said long-term interest rates remain one of the most important variables for bitcoin's next move.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Gold|https://www.kitco.com/news/ar.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Gold is caught between rising yields and safe-haven demand. Prices are near $4,120, up $99 from yesterday but down from January's record. Central bank buying continues, with China adding 23 tonnes in September. The tug-of-war leaves no clear direction today.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Gold rises $99 as yields hit 24-year high",
