    },
    {
      "headline": "99acres and matchmaking grow, Shiksha hit by AI search",
      "essay": "99acres billings rose 21.2% to ₹148.3 crore, accelerating from 16.5% growth in the previous quarter. SimilarWeb data cited by the company showed 99acres held 49% of category web time-share and 59% of app time-share in July and August 2026.\n\nMatchmaking also grew. Jeevansathi billings rose 18.4% to ₹39.7 crore, and Aisle jumped 40%. Combined, the matchmaking vertical grew 23.1%.\n\nShiksha was the weak spot. Billings fell 27.2% to ₹20.5 crore. Info Edge said AI-powered search tools now answer user queries directly, cutting referral traffic to the site. The company is adapting Shiksha's model to reduce search dependence, but expects growth to stay constrained in the interim. For H1 FY27, Shiksha billings were down 24.5% to ₹55.1 crore.\n\nThe pattern is clear: verticals that embed AI into the core user experience are growing, while one that depends on organic search traffic is shrinking.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "AI-Rex and Talent Pulse see rising adoption",
      "essay": "Info Edge said adoption of its B2B AI products, AI-Rex and Talent Pulse, is increasing. Both recorded growth in new customer sign-ups and revenue contribution from new and existing customers.\n\nThese are early-stage products, and the company did not break out their billings. But they sit inside the recruitment segment, which grew 12.6% to ₹613.8 crore. The company is positioning AI-first tools as a way to lift recruitment growth without hurting unit economics.\n\nFor a holder, the signal is that Info Edge is trying to move beyond a legacy job-board model. The numbers are not yet large enough to change the overall growth rate, but they are the piece to watch for whether AI investment translates into higher net retention.",
