topic|Bitcoin|https://www.coindesk.com/m.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed. Bitcoin fell under $81,000 Thursday on oil and Iran fears, then recovered to $82,000 after Trump ruled out a strike before the Nov. 3 midterms. Nearly $974 million in crypto positions were liquidated, $896 million of them longs. Long-term holder buying is down 96% from its September peak, and spot ETFs lost $731 million on Oct. 7-8. The rebound is real, but the buyers behind it are thin.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Bitcoin slides below $81K, then rebounds to $82K on Trump Iran comments",
      "essay": "Bitcoin touched an intraday low of $80,900 on Thursday, then recovered to around $82,000 on Friday after President Donald Trump said the U.S. would not attack Iran before the Nov. 3 midterm elections. Trump called talks with Iran \"productive\" but said the blockade would stay \"in full force and effect.\"\n\nThe selloff started about 24 hours earlier on fears of renewed U.S.-Iran military escalation, which pushed oil higher. Axios reported Oct. 7 that the Pentagon told U.S. Central Command to prepare for resuming major combat operations in Iran. WTI crude rose from $89 to $93.20, then fell back to $90.69 after Trump's post.\n\nThe drop triggered $974 million in crypto liquidations, according to CoinGlass, with $896 million in long positions. Ethereum led at $311 million, Bitcoin at $238 million. Crypto equities fell too: Strategy, Coinbase and Block each dropped over 2%, while miners Riot Platforms fell more than 9% and Cipher Mining 7.9%.\n\nFor Bitcoin holders, the geopolitical trigger has eased, but the market showed how quickly leveraged longs get flushed when oil spikes.",
      "sources": [1, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Long-term holder buying falls 96% as ETF flows turn negative",
      "essay": "Glassnode's Hodler Net Position Change, which tracks the monthly change in coins held by long-term holders, peaked at 25,734 BTC on Sept. 20 and fell 96% to 1,051 BTC by Oct. 8, its weakest positive reading in three months. Holders are still adding, but barely.\n\nETF demand has also cooled. U.S. spot bitcoin ETFs saw $487.1 million in net outflows on Wednesday, led by BlackRock's IBIT at $207.7 million, Fidelity's FBTC at $105.2 million and Ark/21Shares' ARKB at $101.7 million. Funds pulled $731 million on Oct. 7 and 8, per SoSoValue. Over the past five sessions, net outflows total $165.6 million, after a prior nine-day inflow streak of $3.1 billion.\n\nA daily chart signal called a hidden bullish divergence is still intact: Bitcoin made a higher low between Sept. 2 and Oct. 8 while the Relative Strength Index, a 0 to 100 momentum gauge, slid from about 65 to 47. That pattern suggests the uptrend survives a dip. But it needs buyers. A close above $83,325 is the first step; a drop under $80,383 would cancel the signal and put $75,041 back in play.",
      "sources": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "headline": "QCP sees $80,000-$90,000 Q4 range, with Middle East oil as the risk",
      "essay": "QCP Capital's base case puts bitcoin between $80,000 and $90,000 in the fourth quarter. The firm would buy in the $80,000 to $82,000 area and cut exposure around $88,000 to $90,000 if ETF inflows have not picked up. It expects ETF inflows to stay positive but inconsistent, with oil elevated but stable and uncertainty over the Clarity Act still weighing.\n\nThe bull case is above $100,000, driven by sustained ETF inflows, stablecoin supply growth, a Fed pause on weaker employment data, a softer dollar, lower real yields and progress on the Clarity Act. The bear case is below $68,000 to $70,000 if Middle East fighting intensifies, oil rises further and the Fed hikes again, with renewed ETF outflows or a forced corporate holder sale as triggers.\n\nKraken chief economist Thomas Perfumo noted bitcoin's 54% drawdown to its July low is smaller than the 70% to 85% declines of prior cycles, which he credits to spot ETFs, a friendlier regulatory backdrop and crypto's spread into traditional finance. Dates to watch: the Oct. 27-28 Fed meeting, the Nov. 3 midterms and the Nov. 4 Treasury refunding.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

