      "essay": "The Bharat 6G Alliance has grown to more than 90 organisations, including over 30 deep-tech AI companies, chairman NG Subramaniam said at IMC 2026. It started with 10 to 11 members and runs seven working groups covering core technology, applications, spectrum, devices and emerging industries.\n\nSubramaniam, who is also non-executive chairman of Tejas Networks, said government R&D funding for the national 6G push exceeds $10 billion, with India's contribution to next-gen telecom standards at an all-time high. He said early experiments with foundational AI models cut problem-solving cycles by 30 per cent, roughly halving the time to resolve complex network issues.\n\nOn monetisation, he said 6G will go past voice and broadband into intent-based, contextual and compute-driven services, with fee income anchored around identity services, location intelligence, edge AI and sector apps in farming, healthcare and education. For Tejas, the alliance is a channel to standards work and government-funded programmes, though the funding flows to the ecosystem rather than to any single vendor.",
