  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Jio set its IPO band at ₹1,065-1,119 per share, targeting up to ₹30,200 crore and a valuation near $107 billion, below earlier talk of $130-170 billion. The cut shows a weak market: the Nifty 50 is down 14% this year and Reliance shares 23%. Grey market premium of ₹175-180 hints at demand, but the size may pull cash from the secondary market. The final price band is still unconfirmed, and recent large IPOs have had mixed performance. Neutral until the red herring prospectus settles the numbers.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Jio sets IPO price band at ₹1,065-1,119",
      "essay": "Jio Platforms has set its IPO price band at ₹1,065 to ₹1,119 per share. At the top end, the offering would raise about ₹30,200 crore and value Jio at up to ₹10.3 lakh crore, roughly $107 billion. That would make it India's largest IPO ever, beating Hyundai Motor India's ₹27,800 crore issue from October 2024. Jio is selling up to 270 million new shares, or about 2.93% of post-issue equity, and has mandated 19 banks including Kotak Mahindra Capital, Morgan Stanley, Bank of America, Axis Capital, BNP Paribas and Citigroup.\n\nThe anchor book opens on October 19. Subscription runs from October 21 to 23, and trading is expected to start on October 28. Those dates and the price range could still change. The valuation target is lower than before. Earlier talk pointed to ₹11 lakh crore, and figures ranging from $130 billion to $170 billion had circulated. Brokerage estimates in June put Jio at $115-118 billion from Motilal Oswal and around $110 billion from Dolat Capital. A selloff in Indian equities has tempered pricing across several IPOs.\n\nFor anyone holding Jio, the price band is the first real look at what the market will pay for the business. It also sets a public benchmark for the roughly two-thirds stake Reliance Industries owns. Analysts say Reliance shares already reflect a holding-company discount of about 36% on that stake, which some see as too wide. A clean listing price would make that value visible.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Early price talk unsettles Indian equity market",
      "essay": "The Jio IPO chatter is concentrating attention on telecom, digital infrastructure and the new-economy basket, and pulling liquidity toward the primary market instead of secondary stocks. Grey market premium on Jio's unlisted shares stood at ₹175-180 as of October 9, implying about a 16% listing gain over the upper band, according to Business Today. That is not an exchange-traded signal and comes with no guarantee. It does show how fast sentiment can build around a Reliance-backed asset.\n\nReporting on the issue size is inconsistent. One stream says Jio seeks up to ₹3,020 crore; another says proceeds of up to ₹27,500 crore are earmarked to prepay borrowings at subsidiary Reliance Jio Infocomm. The numbers cannot be reconciled until the red herring prospectus is filed, expected after October 12. Price bands cited across outlets also differ, with some referencing ₹1,150-1,220 and ₹1,350-1,450, which adds to the noise.\n\nWhile the paperwork is pending, the market is trading on anticipation. The final prospectus should settle the price band, the record date for the reserved shareholder category, and how much fresh capital actually goes into the business. Until then, holding decisions rest on unconfirmed numbers.",
      "sources": [5]
    }
  ]
}

