  "view": "Flipkart's festive sale opened strong: 6.3 million concurrent users in a minute, 3.5x quick-commerce orders, and 73% tablet growth. Non-metros drove 60% of demand, showing reach. The FSSAI notice is a minor regulatory risk, not a demand issue.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Flipkart Big Billion Days opens with record traffic and quick-commerce surge",
      "essay": "Flipkart's 13th Big Billion Days sale opened with 6.3 million concurrent users in a single minute and over 287 million sessions during early access. Shoppers on the platform during the first hour were more than six times the number in a regular hour.\n\nQuick-commerce service Flipkart Minutes saw orders rise 3.5 times over a regular day, with new customers up 11 times. An iPhone 17 was delivered to a customer in New Delhi in 3.4 minutes during early access. Tablets grew 73% year on year, large appliances 30%, and beauty 42%. One in four mobile customers used exchange, and one in three chose no-cost EMI.\n\nNon-metro demand was strong: nearly half of first-hour visits came from Tier 3 towns and beyond, and non-metros accounted for about 60% of demand. New customers rose 34%. The opening figures show Flipkart's reach beyond metros and its ability to handle high traffic, though the reported delivery time is a single example, not a typical speed.",
      "sources": [3, 4, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "FSSAI issues notices to Flipkart and others over Celevida GLP+ claims",
      "essay": "The Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) issued show-cause notices to Dr. Reddy's Laboratories, Nestlé Health Science, and e-commerce platforms including Flipkart, Amazon India, Netmeds, and contract manufacturer Tirupati Wellness over allegedly misleading advertising and unapproved health claims for Celevida GLP+. The notices were announced on October 8, 2026.\n\nFSSAI flagged statements such as \"High protein support for lean muscle preservation during GLP-1/GIP therapies\" and \"imported from Europe for purity,\" and questioned whether the brand name \"Celevida GLP+\" creates a deceptive association between a dietary supplement and clinical drug regimens. The product launched in May 2026 under a joint venture between Dr. Reddy's and Nestlé Health Science.\n\nThe notices require formal explanations before FSSAI decides on further enforcement, product relabeling, or ad withdrawals. For Flipkart, this is a regulatory compliance issue on its marketplace, not a direct sales or demand problem.",
      "sources": [6]
    }
  ]
