topic|Flipkart|https://www.linkedin.com/.text:
{
  "stance": "bullish",
  "view": "Flipkart's 13th Big Billion Days opened strong: 6.3 million concurrent users in a single minute, over 287 million early-access sessions, and new customers up 34%. Quick commerce is the standout, with Flipkart Minutes orders up 3.5x and new customers up 11x, including an iPhone 17 delivered in 3.4 minutes. Tier 3 towns drove nearly half of first-hour visits and about 60% of demand. The numbers are company-reported and cover only the opening, so treat them as momentum, not the full sale.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Flipkart Big Billion Days opens with record traffic",
      "essay": "Flipkart's 13th Big Billion Days opened with 6.3 million concurrent users in a single minute, and more than six times as many Indians shopped on the platform in the first hour as in a regular hour. Early access, which started at midnight on 8 October for eligible members, drew over 287 million sessions. New customers rose 34%.\n\nSmaller markets did much of the work. Nearly half of first-hour visits came from Tier 3 towns and smaller locations, and non-metro areas contributed about 60% of demand. Large appliance sales grew 30% year on year in the opening hour, tablets 73%, and beauty 42%. At peak, Flipkart sold more than 1,600 men's perfumes per minute. One in four mobile customers used exchange, and one in three chose no-cost EMI.\n\nAmazon's Great Indian Festival, which began on 8 October, reported product orders up 100% year on year and customers ordering from every pin code. The two companies measure different periods and categories, so the figures are not a direct comparison. For Flipkart, the opening shows demand is broad and financing-led, but it is still only the first hour of a long sale.",
      "sources": [5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Flipkart Minutes quick commerce surges during festive sale",
      "essay": "Flipkart Minutes, the company's quick-commerce service, saw orders rise 3.5 times over a regular day in the opening hour of The Big Billion Days, with new customers up 11-fold. An iPhone 17 reached a customer in New Delhi in 3.4 minutes during early access. The company notes that delivery time is one example, not a typical speed.\n\nThe service is moving beyond groceries. Shoppers used Minutes for laptops, external hard drives, air fryers, premium electronics, grooming products and wearables. Tier 2-plus cities are among the fastest-growing markets on Minutes.\n\nAmazon said that in more than 120 cities served by Amazon Now, half of the products sold during the reported period were delivered within minutes. Quick commerce is becoming a real channel for high-value electronics, not just daily essentials. For Flipkart, that widens the basket and pulls in new customers, though the 3.4-minute delivery is an outlier.",
      "sources": [4, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Festive discounts hit iPhone 17 and QLED TVs",
      "essay": "Festive pricing is live on both platforms. The Apple iPhone 17, which launched in 2025 with a 6.3-inch 120Hz OLED display and A19 chip, is selling as low as Rs 76,999 after a base price hike to Rs 99,900. Amazon and Flipkart are both discounting it during their sales.\n\nTelevisions are also marked down. A 55-inch Samsung Neo QLED 4K is listed at Rs 67,990 on Amazon, down from Rs 99,900. Xiaomi's 55-inch Smartchoice FX Pro QLED is Rs 35,499 on Amazon against Rs 62,999. On Flipkart, Acerpure's 55-inch QLED is Rs 37,999 from Rs 80,990, and Motorola's 55-inch QLED is Rs 31,999 from Rs 66,999. Prices can change with bank discounts and exchange offers.\n\nFlipkart's supply chain team is highlighting the work behind these deliveries during the sale. The discounts are pulling in shoppers, but the final price depends on offers stacked at checkout.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3]
    }
  ]
