  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed day. The IPO is real and large: Rs 30,213 crore raise, $106 billion valuation, subscription October 21-23. But that valuation sits about 19% below the $131 billion floated in June, and Musk is publicly attacking the approval process days before the issue. Strong cash generation and a lower net leverage ratio of 0.36 times in FY26 support the story. Regulatory noise and the price reset are the offset.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Jio IPO targets $106 billion valuation, below June expectation",
      "essay": "Jio Platforms is seeking a valuation of about $106 billion, or Rs 10.3 lakh crore, in an IPO that could raise up to Rs 30,213 crore at a price band of Rs 1,065 to Rs 1,119 per share. That would beat Hyundai Motor India's Rs 27,858.75 crore issue in 2024 as India's largest ever.\n\nThe number is a reset. Reuters reported in June that the expectation was about $131 billion, so the target is roughly 19% lower. Analysts cited by Reuters tied the cut to pressure on Indian equities from an oil-price shock and geopolitical uncertainty. The issue is a fresh sale of up to 27 crore shares with no offer-for-sale component, so the money goes to the company, not exiting shareholders. Up to Rs 27,500 crore of net proceeds is earmarked to repay or prepay borrowings of Reliance Jio Infocomm.\n\nThe timetable: anchor investors on October 19, public subscription October 21 to 23, listing possibly October 28. These dates are reported plans, not final confirmed terms. The draft prospectus leaves price and issue-period fields blank. For anyone holding the topic, the lower valuation improves the entry point versus June, but it does not by itself make the offer cheap. That depends on earnings, debt and what investors pay for the digital businesses.",
      "sources": [3, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Musk attacks Jio and Modi government over Starlink delay",
      "essay": "Elon Musk said SpaceX must set affordable prices for Starlink in India, where it plans to compete with Reliance Jio. He did not name prices. Mobile internet in India costs about 8 cents per gigabyte, among the lowest rates in the world, and cheap tariffs were the foundation of Jio's growth.\n\nMusk went further, accusing the government of Prime Minister Narendra Modi and Mukesh Ambani of delaying Starlink's entry to favour Jio. He mocked Ambani as \"Prime Minister Ambani\" and asked whether he would allow Starlink to compete. The Indian government rejected the bias claim, saying all three satellite projects awaiting launch, including Starlink and Jio's own, still lack final security clearance. Reliance did not comment.\n\nThe timing matters. The posts landed days before Jio Platforms' IPO, and Musk's shift is sharp given his previously warm relations with Modi. For the topic, this is noise around the listing rather than a change in Jio's business. Starlink and Jio's satellite arm are stuck at the same approval gate, so neither has a head start.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jio enters IPO with stronger cash, lower leverage",
      "essay": "Jio Platforms' net leverage ratio, a measure of debt against earnings, fell from 0.88 times in FY24 to 0.36 times in FY26. Revenue from operations rose from Rs 1.10 lakh crore to Rs 1.47 lakh crore over the same period, and profit after tax climbed from Rs 21,423 crore to Rs 30,049 crore. Cash left after capital expenditure jumped from Rs 1,449 crore to Rs 42,071 crore.\n\nThe company is shifting from adding customers to earning more from the network it built. Average monthly revenue per user rose from Rs 181.7 to Rs 214, while monthly data use grew from 28.7 GB to 42.3 GB. Fixed broadband reached 27.1 million customers, a 42.6% market share. Jio has filed 6,817 patent applications, with 1,009 granted globally, and is pushing its 5G and pre-6G stack to overseas operators.\n\nAn Ambit Capital analyst said the IPO should move Jio toward a more conservative capital structure, with the next phase defined by monetisation and spending discipline. For the topic, the balance sheet is the strongest part of the pitch going into the offer.",
      "sources": [5]
    }
  ]
}

