mover|up|https://energy.economictimes.in.text:
{"headline": "KEC International jumps 5.79% on ₹1,014 crore transmission order win", "essay": "KEC International builds infrastructure on contract. That sector, called EPC (engineering, procurement and construction), means a company designs a project, buys the material and builds it for a client, usually a government body or utility. In KEC's case the work is power transmission lines, railway electrification, civil construction, solar and cables. EPC firms earn a fixed margin on the contract value, so order flow is the number that drives the stock.

KEC is the RPG Group's flagship infrastructure arm, with operations in more than 110 countries. Power transmission and distribution is its biggest vertical, and it also does railways, urban infrastructure, solar, oil and gas pipelines, and cables and conductors. It reported ₹5,024 crore of sales in the June 2026 quarter, down from ₹6,390 crore in March 2026, with operating profit of ₹291 crore and a 6% operating margin. Net profit for that quarter was ₹73 crore, or ₹2.73 per share, against ₹193 crore and ₹7.24 in the March quarter. The fall is sharp, and the company's own quarterly profit change of -41.72% in the peer table confirms it. At ₹370, the stock trades at 16.4 times earnings and 1.6 times book value of ₹231, with a 1.49% dividend yield and 16.5% ROCE, a measure of how efficiently capital is used. Peers are priced higher: Larsen & Toubro at 28.9 times earnings, Kalpataru Projects at 21.4 and Rail Vikas at 44.2. KEC's 16.4 is low for the group, though its 11.4% ROE is also below Kalpataru's 18.28% ROCE and NBCC's 29.32%. Screener flags a low three-year average ROE of 10.8% as a weakness, and a healthy 26.5% dividend payout as a strength. The stock sits far below its 52-week high of ₹894 and just above its low of ₹348.

The move followed the order announcement. On 10 October 2026, KEC said it won ₹1,014 crore of India transmission and distribution orders, including its first 765 kV transmission line, built to carry power away from a pumped storage plant in western India. Pumped storage is a hydro plant that pumps water uphill when power is cheap and releases it to generate when demand peaks. The 765 kV figure is the voltage class, and higher voltage lines move more power over longer distances. Year-to-date order intake is now above ₹9,600 crore. Management said the order came from an existing client and supports renewable power infrastructure. The stock closed at ₹365.75 that day, up 4.62%, and the 5.79% gain in the latest session extends that run. No other explanation for the move appears in the articles."}

