      "headline": "Ex-Flipkart staff press Walmart on ESOP liquidity",
      "essay": "More than 1,000 former employees of the Flipkart group have appealed to Walmart and Flipkart's board for an exit on their vested stock options. The group spans Flipkart, Myntra, Cleartrip, Shopsy, Supermoney and ANS. A petition dated October 10 gathered over 500 signatures within 24 hours and aims to rally 20,000 to 30,000 former staff.\n\nThree demands: extend the liquidity offered to current employees to eligible ex-employees, extend the post-exit exercise window for vested options, and appoint a point of contact for talks. The move follows an October 1 letter from former senior executives, including ex-Myntra CEO Mukesh Bansal and ex-CFO Sanjay Baweja, seeking \"fair and equitable treatment.\" Walmart's counsel said former employees will be considered at the IPO, without a firm commitment.\n\nMoney is the pressure point. Current and former employees stand to share about $4 billion (roughly Rs 38,000 crore) from buybacks Walmart has not yet run. Former employees account for roughly half, about $2 billion. Flipkart was valued at $38.2 billion in July's buyback, up 6 per cent from $36 billion in May 2024, when eligible staff could sell up to 5 per cent of vested options. The company targets an IPO valuation of about $50 billion. For anyone holding Flipkart exposure, the signal is governance friction: the people who built the company are fighting for the same exit that public-market investors are waiting on.",
