{"headline": "PC market witnessed a massive contraction in Q3 of 2026: Gartner", "essay": "Memory got expensive, so people stopped buying computers. Worldwide PC shipments fell 22.4% year over year in the third quarter of 2026, to 55.4 million units, Gartner reports. That is a volume last seen in early 2023, but the cause is different. In past slumps, shelves were full of unsold machines. This time the parts cost too much. DRAM prices rose fivefold year over year in the second quarter, and vendors passed the bill to buyers. Average selling prices climbed. Replacements stopped making sense.

Nobody escaped. Lenovo, the market leader, fell 22.4%. HP fell 30.9%, the worst of the top six, because it sells most into price-sensitive consumer and business segments. Apple fell least and gained 1.7 points of share on its MacBook line. Scarce components pushed vendors toward high-priced configurations, so corporate buyers confined spending to AI PCs and workstations instead of broad rollouts.

My view: this is a supply shock wearing a demand shock's clothes. When memory prices normalize, the buyers will still be there."}

